   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to resolution of disputes\nin the course of collective negotiations",
    "basePrintNo": "S5737",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5737-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 55,
       "fullName": "Rich Funke",
       "imgName": "887_rich_funke.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 887,
       "sessionMemberId": 1367,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "FUNKE"
      },
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 59,
       "fullName": "Patrick M. Gallivan",
       "imgName": "427_patrick_m._gallivan.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 427,
       "sessionMemberId": 1417,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "GALLIVAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 2
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §209, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5737\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the civil service law, in relation to\nresolution of disputes in the course of collective negotiations\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS: OF BILL:\n\nThis proposal amends S 208 (5) (a) of the civil service law by adding\nthe four upstate transit authorities to the binding arbitration\nprovisions included in this section\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent law requires any contract disputes between the New York City\nTransit Authority (NYCTA) and the Metropolitan Transit Authority (MTA)\nand its employees to be referred to a public arbitration panel to\nresolve impasses in collective negotiations. This bill would expand the\nprovisions of this law to include the employees of the upstate transit\nauthorities the Niagara Frontier Transportation Authority, the Roches-\nter-Genesee Regional Transportation Authority, the Capital District\nTransportation Authority and the Central New York Regional Transporta-\ntion Authority.\n\nCompulsory arbitration has allowed the people of New York to enjoy unin-\nterrupted service in the MTA and NYCTA area since 1989. All evaluations\nof the effects of this law have been positive and have advocated the\ncontinuation of this process. Failure to expand this provision to the\nupstate transit authorities creates a needless distinction between tran-\nsit workers upstate and transit workers downstate.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.4746/A.5303 of 2015-2016;Referred to Rules\nS.7729/A.9419 of 2014; Referred to Civil Service and Pensions\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided, however, that the\namendments to paragraph (a) of subdivision 5 of section 209 of the civil\nservice law made by section one of this act shall not affect the expira-\ntion of such subdivision and shall be deemed to expire therewith.\n",
