 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Requires the Niagara Frontier Transportation Authority, the Rochester-Genesee Regional Transportation Authority, the Capital District Transportation Authority and the Central New York Regional Transportation Authority and their employees to submit all unresolvable contract negotiations to binding arbitration.",
 "title": "Requires various public transit authorities and their employees to submit all unresolvable contract negotiations to binding arbitration",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S5737",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S5737-2019",
     "printNo": "S5737",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 227\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 5737, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to resolution of\n      disputes in the course of collective negotiations\"\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 5386, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the civil service law, in relation to employees\n      classified as the Triborough bridge and tunnel authority  superior\n      officers  benevolent association of an MTA-employer and the resol-\n      ution of disputes in the course of collective negotiations\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  These bills would allow Metropolitan  Transportation  Authority  (MTA)\nemployees  represented  by  the Triborough Bridge and Tunnel Authority's\nSuperior Officers Benevolent Association (SOBA) and employees of upstate\ntransit authorities to be covered by the binding arbitration  provisions\nof Section 209 of the Civil Service Law.\n \n  When  Section  209 was enacted, SOBA affirmatively chose not to opt-in\nto compulsory arbitration to resolve impasses in collective  bargaining.\nSigning  this  bill  would  establish  a  precedent  and encourage other\nrepresented employees who did not elect binding arbitration  to  attempt\nto do so now.\n \n  Bills  similar  to  these  have been vetoed in the past. Binding arbi-\ntration undermines the public employer's bargaining ability, which ulti-\nmately drives up labor costs and hurts New York State taxpayers.  Unnec-\nessarily  ceding  a  final  decision over compensation and benefits to a\nthird party will result in a loss  of  oversight  and  control  for  all\ninvolved parties.\n\n  Furthermore,  these bills would provide binding arbitration as a means\nof resolving bargaining disputes where the collective bargaining process\ncan sufficiently address the needs of employers and employees. Absent  a\ncompelling  need  for  this process, which the sponsors have not identi-\nfied, I am constrained to veto these bills.\n \n  These bills are disapproved.                (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
